<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
  Severity overrides for Android Lint. app/build.gradle.kts sets warningsAsErrors, so anything left at
  WARNING here fails the build — keep this file to the checks that are deliberately, permanently
  exempt, and fix everything else at the source.
-->
<lint>
    <!--
      VectorPath fires on five paths over lint's 3000-character limit, all of them brand art:
      drawable/thor_mono.xml (three paths), drawable/thor_drawn_foreground.xml and
      drawable/dhizuku.xml. #269 already compacted every vector path that could be shortened
      losslessly; what remains is real curve detail, so lint's two suggestions — drop precision, or
      rasterise — would either visibly change the logo or break the adaptive/monochrome icon sources,
      which have to stay vectors.

      Downgraded to informational rather than ignored: this keeps a *newly* oversized path visible in
      the lint report instead of silently accepting it, while warningsAsErrors only promotes WARNING,
      so it cannot fail the build.
    -->
    <issue id="VectorPath" severity="informational" />

    <!--
      Both of these report "a newer version of X is available" — GradleDependency on the release
      variants, NewerVersionAvailable on the debug ones (it also covers plugins, which is how the
      Koin compiler plugin turned up). That is a notice about the outside world, not a defect in
      this repo: with warningsAsErrors it turns every upstream release into a red build here, on a
      commit that changed nothing. The catalog deliberately pins fast-moving pre-release artifacts
      (AGP 9.4.0-alpha06, material3 1.5.0-alphaNN, navigation3, androidx-adaptive rc), so that is
      not hypothetical — between adding this block and running lint twice it fired on material3
      alpha25, navigation3 1.1.5 and koin-compiler 1.1.0.

      Same reasoning that took the Play upload off every master push: a check that goes red when
      you have done nothing wrong stops being a signal and trains you to ignore red. Dependency
      currency is a deliberate, reviewed bump, not a build gate.

      Informational rather than ignored, so the upgrade candidates stay listed in the report.
    -->
    <issue id="GradleDependency" severity="informational" />
    <issue id="NewerVersionAvailable" severity="informational" />

    <!--
      AndroidGradlePluginVersion is the same notice about AGP itself, and it is here for the same
      reason with one difference worth naming: it ships at ERROR, so warningsAsErrors never entered
      into it and the two overrides above did not cover it. AGP 9.4.0-alpha07 published on
      2026-07-30 and every open PR went red within the hour — five branches failing build-and-test
      on a line none of them touched.

      Pinning an alpha and being told an hour later that a newer alpha exists is not news; it is the
      cost of pinning an alpha on purpose. The bump itself stays a reviewed change, as it already
      was: a pre-release AGP moves the toolchain under a build that has to stay reproducible for
      IzzyOnDroid, so it is exactly the kind of thing that should arrive in its own commit rather
      than be forced by whichever CI run happened to notice.
    -->
    <issue id="AndroidGradlePluginVersion" severity="informational" />

    <!--
      SdCardPath exists to stop production code hardcoding a storage path. A test that *asserts about*
      a path has to contain the literal to do its job — e.g. PerUserCommandsTest's
      `assertFalse(path.startsWith("/data/data/"))`, which is checking Thor does NOT emit a per-user
      alias. Scoped rather than downgraded, so the check keeps full force everywhere it is meaningful:
      this is the exact inversion of the rule's intent, not a rule we disagree with.

      Nine hits, all of this shape, are what `checkTestSources = true` surfaced when it was turned on
      (see the comment on that line in build.gradle.kts). Without this scope the flag cannot be
      adopted; with it, the test tree is analysed at a net cost of zero findings.
    -->
    <issue id="SdCardPath">
        <ignore path="src/test/**" />
    </issue>

    <!--
      SyntheticAccessor is the one check in this file that is being turned *on*: it ships
      enabledByDefault = false, so naming a severity here is what enables it, and warningsAsErrors
      then makes it fatal. :bypass took the equivalent step in its own build.gradle.kts lint block;
      :app puts it here because that is where this module keeps a rule's reason next to the rule.

      Measured on :app:lintStoreRelease before the fixes: 62 findings from 14 declarations. Eight are
      top-level `private` declarations reached from a class in the same file — the file facade
      (FooKt) is a separate JVM class, so every such reference costs an access$ bridge — and six are
      `private` class members reached from a lambda or coroutine body that compiles to its own class.
      All 14 are now `internal`, which is where the count came from: 33 for dataStore and 8 for
      SUSPEND_USER_ID alone. Counted for storeRelease, with checkTestSources = true and the store
      source set in scope; the doc's older "43 main / 51 storeDebug" was measured 2026-08-07 and the
      module has grown since. Do not quote a SyntheticAccessor count without naming the variant.

      What it is worth, measured rather than assumed: the *release* dex holds 21 access$ methods out
      of 30,682 total, against 3,563 in storeDebug — R8 inlines almost all of them. Only three of the
      62 findings named a bridge that actually survives to the shipped APK (ThorRootService's
      clearAppData, dumpPackage and setAppSuspendedAs, kept verbatim because the class is), and the
      other 18 survivors belong to Odin, which checkDependencies = false puts out of scope anyway. So
      the follow-up doc's "method count against the 64K limit" framing overstates this by ~20x. It is
      enabled regardless, for the reason it is enabled everywhere else here: a debug build carries
      every one of those bridges, and pinning at zero is cheap while re-privatising later is not.
    -->
    <issue id="SyntheticAccessor" severity="warning" />
</lint>
